# Why are some countries rich and others poor?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why did living standards stay flat for millennia and then shoot up like a hockey stick? Why do the gaps between countries today dwarf anything in history?"
next: {"title":"What is it that is scarce?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/02-what-is-it-that-is-scarce.cast"}

## The hockey stick
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 1
      - 1000
      - 1500
      - 1600
      - 1700
      - 1820
      - 1850
      - 1870
      - 1900
      - 1920
      - 1940
      - 1950
      - 1960
      - 1970
      - 1980
      - 1990
      - 1991
      - 1992
      - 1993
      - 1994
      - 1995
      - 1996
      - 1997
      - 1998
      - 1999
      - 2000
      - 2001
      - 2002
      - 2003
      - 2004
      - 2005
      - 2006
      - 2007
      - 2008
      - 2009
      - 2010
      - 2011
      - 2012
      - 2013
      - 2014
      - 2015
      - 2016
      - 2017
      - 2018
      - 2019
      - 2020
      - 2021
      - 2022
    series:
      - {name: World, values: [1100, 1100, 1300, 1400, 1400, 1500, 1800, 2100, 3100, 3400, 4700, 4600, 6000, 8200, 9900, 11275, 11183, 11148, 11138, 11265, 11443, 11692, 11980, 12098, 12361, 12781, 12915, 13104, 13411, 13921, 14377, 14935, 15520, 15756, 15457, 16051, 16479, 16785, 17122, 17503, 17871, 18234, 18715, 19189, 19551, 18812, 19876, 20422]}
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 22000
    x_label: Year
    y_label: GDP per person ($, 2011 prices)
sources:
    - id: maddison2023
      title: Maddison Project Database 2023 (via Our World in Data)
      authors: Bolt, J. & van Zanden, J. L.
      year: 2024
      finding: 'GDP per person in international dollars at 2011 prices: world about $1,100 in year 1 and 1000, $1,500 in 1820, $20,400 in 2022'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/gdp-per-capita-maddison-project-database

    dot p1 at.data [1,1100] hidden true
    label p1_label "$1,100" above-right attach_to p1 hidden true
    dot p1820 at.data [1820,1500] hidden true
    label p1820_label "$1,500" above-left attach_to p1820 hidden true
    dot p2022 at.data [2022,20400] cites ["maddison2023"] hidden true
    label p2022_label "$20,400" left attach_to p2022 hidden true
    text handle "handle" at.data [900,4200] font_size 30 hidden true
    text blade "blade" at.data [1780,12000] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "The hockey stick"

Why are some countries rich and others poor? To see how strange that question is, start with the whole world, two thousand years ago.
    draw axes

This is average income per person, for the whole planet, in dollars adjusted for prices.
    highlight axes

In the year one, it was about eleven hundred dollars a year: roughly what people in the poorest countries live on today.
    draw p1 p1_label

B: Let me guess: it crept up slowly, century after century.
    point at.data [1000,4000] gesture tap

    ask "Draw the line on, from the year 1000 to today." on line_1 from 1000 store g relative true
        default: 1150,1800,1930,2060,2216,2255,2281,2320,2346,2372,2385,2398,2411,2424,2437,2438,2440,2441,2442,2444,2445,2446,2447,2449,2450,2451,2453,2454,2455,2456,2458,2459,2460,2462,2463,2464,2466,2467,2468,2470,2471,2472,2473,2475,2476,2477,2479
        intro: Draw your own guess first, from the year one thousand to today.
        right: Close: on average you were {g.pct} off.
        wrong: On average you were {g.pct} off. The real line hugs the floor for centuries, then leaps.

Here is the estimate: still about eleven (@point at.data [1000,1100] gesture tap duration 1.5@) hundred in the year one thousand, and only fifteen hu (@draw p1820 p1820_label@)ndred by 1820, after eighteen centuries.

B: So for almost all of history, a child grew up about as poor as their great-grandparents.
    highlight p1 p1820

Then the line turns: twenty-one hundred by 1870, forty-six hundred by 1950,
    blocking false

    point at.data [1870,2100] gesture tap duration 1.5

    point at.data [1950,4600] gesture tap duration 1.5

and about twenty thousand today, more than thirteen times the level of 1820.
    draw p2022 p2022_label

    scratch readout at.data [800,15000] work ["×13.6 since 1820"] font_size 30

Economists call this shape the hockey stick (@draw handle@): a long flat handle, t (@draw blade@)hen a blade.

B: How can anyone know incomes in the year one thousand?
    erase readout

Not precisely: these are reconstructions from wages, prices and harvest records. But even a large error would not bend the handle much.
    highlight handle effect underline

So the first puzzle is not why some countries are poor. Poverty was normal. The puzzle is why, after 1800, some places stopped being poor.
    highlight blade effect circle

    pause 0.8

## The great divergence
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 1820
      - 1870
      - 1913
      - 1929
      - 1950
      - 1960
      - 1970
      - 1980
      - 1990
      - 2000
      - 2010
      - 2022
    series:
      - {name: Britain, values: [3306, 5829, 8212, 8772, 11061, 13780, 17162, 20612, 26189, 31946, 34754, 38406]}
      - {name: United States, values: [2674, 4803, 10107, 11954, 15240, 18057, 23958, 29611, 36982, 45886, 49266, 58487]}
      - {name: Norway, values: [1384, 2177, 4415, 5581, 8655, 11483, 15983, 24031, 29434, 54039, 78476, 88366]}
      - {name: China, values: [882, 945, 985, 1003, 799, 1057, 1398, 1930, 2982, 4730, 9658, 19238]}
      - {name: South Korea, values: [816, 820, 1171, 1495, 998, 1547, 2974, 6064, 13874, 23108, 31537, 41321]}
      - {name: Burundi, values: [null, null, null, null, 595, 733, 893, 952, 1124, 739, 717, 717]}
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 95000
    x_label: Year
    y_label: GDP per person ($, 2011 prices)
sources:
    - id: maddison2023
      title: Maddison Project Database 2023 (via Our World in Data)
      authors: Bolt, J. & van Zanden, J. L.
      year: 2024
      finding: 'GDP per person, international dollars at 2011 prices, 1820 → 2022: UK 3,306 → 38,406; US 2,674 → 58,487; Norway 1,384 → 88,366; China 882 → 19,238; South Korea 816 → 41,321; Burundi 2022: 717. Widest 1820 gap among 54 countries ≈ 5:1 (UK vs Nepal)'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/gdp-per-capita-maddison-project-database

    scratch c1820 cites ["maddison2023"] work ["1820","Britain $3,300","China $880","Korea $820"] x 420 y 500 hidden true
    scratch ratio cites ["maddison2023"] work ["Burundi 2022: $717","Norway ÷ Burundi ≈ 123","1820: widest gap ≈ 5 : 1"] x 420 y 500 hidden true

    card title "The great divergence"

The blade did not rise everywhere at once. Here are a few countries in 1820 and in 2022, in the same fixed dollars.
    draw axes

B: Who was richest in 1820?
    point at.data [1820,4000] gesture tap duration 1.5

Britain, with (@draw c1820_box c1820_line_1 c1820_line_2@) about thirty-three hundred dollars a head. China and Korea had (@draw c1820_line_3 c1820_line_4@) eight or nine hundred: a gap of about four to one.

B: And two centuries later the gap is still about four to one?
    erase c1820

Not even close. Britai (@draw line_1@)n reached about thirty-eight thousand, the United Sta (@draw line_2@)tes fifty-eight thousand, and (@draw line_3@) Norway, with its oil, eighty-eight thousand.

China climbed to about nineteen thousand, most of it since 1980.
    draw line_4

B: And Korea?
    fade line_1 line_2 line_3 line_4 to 0.3

Among the poorest in 1820 and still poor in 1950, South Korea passed forty-one thousand.
    draw line_5

Meanwhile, av (@draw line_6@)erage income in Burundi in 2022 was about seven hu (@draw ratio_box ratio_line_1@)ndred dollars: below the world average two thousand years ago.

B: So Norway t (@draw ratio_line_2@)o Burundi is now more than a hundred to one, where the widest gap (@draw ratio_line_3@)s in 1820 were about five to one.

Historians c (@erase ratio@)all this widening the Gre (@fade line_1 line_2 line_3 line_4 to 1@)at Divergence. And Korea shows the other half of t (@highlight line_5@)he puzzle: being poor is not destiny.

    pause 0.8

## Small rates, huge gaps
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation: y = 1000*(1 + g)^n
    variable: 'n'
    params:
      g: {value: 0.01, min: 0, max: 0.03, step: 0.001, label: growth rate g}
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 200
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 60000
    x_label: years
    y_label: $ per person
    marks:
      - {kind: point, at: 200, label: false, id: end}

    text m7 "×7.3" at.data [180,11500] font_size 28 hidden true
    dot g14 at.data [200,14600] hidden true
    label g14_label "×14?" left attach_to g14 hidden true
    text m52 "×52" at.data [180,55000] font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch rule work ["70 ÷ 2 = 35 years","to double"] x 400 y 320 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch mth work [{"tex":"(1+g)^T = 2"},{"tex":"T\\,\\ln(1+g) = \\ln 2 \\approx 0.69"},{"tex":"\\ln(1+g) \\approx g"},{"tex":"T \\approx \\frac{0.69}{g} \\approx \\frac{70}{100\\,g}"}] x 400 y 320 hidden true

    card title "Small rates, huge gaps"

How can gaps grow that wide in only two centuries?
    draw axes

    pause 0.4

B: Small growth rates, repeated. Take a c (@draw eq curve_0@)ountry at a thousand dollars a head, an illustrative number, growing one per cent a year.

B: After two hundred years it is about seven times richer.
    draw end m7

And at two per cent? Twice the rate, so fourteen times, I suppose.
    draw g14 g14_label

B: Keep a ghost of the one per cent path, and watch. After the same two hundred years: fifty-two times richer.
    animate params.g.value 0.02 duration 3.5 ghost true

So one extra percentage point turns seven into fifty-two.
    draw m52

    erase g14 g14_label

B: A handy rule: divide seventy by the growth rate to get the years it takes to double.  (@draw rule_box rule_line_1@)At two per cent, that is thirty (@draw rule_line_2@)-five years: about twice in a lifetime.

    erase rule

B: Drag the growth rate yourself. Try three per cent, and watch where the line ends.
    explore

That is why economists obsess over a single percentage point: over a few generations it is the difference between staying poor and getting rich.
    highlight eq_param_g

B: If you want to see where the seventy comes from, open the math; if not, skip it.
    draw mathq

    ask "Where the rule of 70 comes from: open the math, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own voice b

@math

    erase mathq

If you chose the math: doubling means one plus g, multiplied T times, equals two.
    draw mth_box mth_line_1

Take logs: T times the log of one plus g equals the log of two, about 0.69.
    draw mth_line_2

For small g, the log of one plus g is close to g itself.
    draw mth_line_3

So T is about 0.69 over g: seventy over the rate in per cent.
    draw mth_line_4

    pause 1

    erase mth

@after

    erase mathq

    pause 0.5

## Four suspects
sources:
    - id: ajr2001
      title: 'The Colonial Origins of Comparative Development: An Empirical Investigation'
      authors: Acemoglu, D., Johnson, S. & Robinson, J. A.
      year: 2001
      finding: Where colonisers set up extractive institutions, countries are poorer today; institutions explain much of the income gap between former colonies
    - id: sachs2003
      title: 'Institutions Don''t Rule: Direct Effects of Geography on Per Capita Income'
      authors: Sachs, J. D. (NBER Working Paper 9490)
      year: 2003
      finding: Malaria risk lowers income per person even controlling for the quality of institutions
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w9490
    - id: maddison2023
      title: Maddison Project Database 2023 (via Our World in Data)
      authors: Bolt, J. & van Zanden, J. L.
      year: 2024
      finding: 'GDP per person 2022 (2011 $): South Korea 41,321, North Korea 1,569 (a rough estimate)'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/gdp-per-capita-maddison-project-database

    text q "2 % a year — and keep it up?" center font_size 40 hidden true
    box cap "Capital" x 330 y 500 width 250 height 160 radius 10 icon factory font_size 30 hidden true
    box ideas "Ideas" x 610 y 500 width 250 height 160 radius 10 icon "light bulb" font_size 30 hidden true
    box inst "Institutions" cites ["ajr2001"] x 330 y 310 width 250 height 160 radius 10 icon "classical building" font_size 30 hidden true
    box geo "Geography & culture" cites ["sachs2003"] x 610 y 310 width 250 height 160 radius 10 icon globe font_size 28 hidden true
    group suspects members ["cap","ideas","inst","geo"] hidden true
    text how "how" x 140 y 470 font_size 34 hidden true
    text why "why" x 140 y 270 font_size 34 hidden true
    portrait acemoglu x 870 y 420 width 200 of "Daron Acemoglu" cameo true hidden true
    box facts "Facts: consensus" x 870 y 500 width 220 height 90 font_size 26 hidden true
    box causes "Causes: contested" x 870 y 310 width 220 height 90 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Four suspects"

So the whole question shrinks to this: why did some places start growing at two per cent a year, and keep it up, while others did not?
    draw q

    pause 0.6

Economists have four main suspects.
    erase q

First, capital: machines, roads and factories, more tools for every worker.
    draw cap

Second, ideas: new technology, from steam engines to vaccines, that makes the same worker produce more.
    draw ideas

Third, institutions: the rules of the game. Who owns what, whether courts are fair, and whether those in power can be removed.
    draw inst

Fourth, geography and culture: climate, disease, access to the sea, and how far people trust strangers.
    draw geo

B: But capital and ideas ar (@draw how@)e just how a country grows. The real fight is ov (@draw why@)er why some countries get them and others don't.

And there the field splits.  (@draw acemoglu@)Daron Acemoglu, Simon Johnson and James Robinson argue that instit (@highlight inst effect box@)utions rule: where colonisers set up rules that served only a few, countries are poorer today.

B: Jeffrey (@erase acemoglu@) Sachs answers that geography also  (@highlight geo effect box@)acts directly: where malaria is rife, incomes are lower, even with the same quality of government.

How sure are we? About the hockey stick and the diverge (@draw facts@)nce: consensus. About the de (@draw causes@)ep causes: still contested.

    ask "Which suspect does the Korean split point to? Tap it." choose ["cap","ideas","inst","geo"] answer inst stage own voice b
        intro: Three questions to try the idea on new cases. In 1945 Korea was split in two: at the split, the same geography, the same culture, the same history. By the best estimates, the South is now more than twenty times richer per person than the North.
        right: Institutions. Geography and culture were shared; what differed after 1945 were the rules: markets and courts in the South, central planning under one party in the North.
        wrong: Which of the four was different on the two sides of the border after 1945?

    quiz "Country A grows 1% a year, country B 3%. Both start at $2,000 a head. After 70 years, roughly how many times richer is B than A?"
        right: About four times. A doubles once in 70 years; B doubles about three times, to eight times its start.
        wrong: Use the rule of 70: count how many times each one doubles in 70 years.
        * About the same
        * About twice
        + About four times
        * About ten times

    quiz "A poor country manages 7% growth a year. Roughly how many years until its income per person doubles?" voice b
        right: About ten: seventy divided by seven.
        wrong: Divide seventy by the growth rate.
        * 7
        + 10
        * 35
        * 70

Rich and poor are not two kinds of country so much as two speeds. The real puzzle, and a thread through this course, is what lets a country find the faster speed and hold it.
    highlight suspects

    pause 1

